<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anshan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as King of Anshan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anshan was an ancient city in the highlands of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, at the site of Tal-e Malyan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>. It was one of the principal centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, whose kings styled themselves rulers of Anshan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>. Later the early Achaemenid rulers, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>, used the title King of Anshan.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Achaemenid Empire, the first Persian Empire, was founded by Cyrus II (Cyrus the Great) following his accession as <b>King of Anshan</b> in Persia around 559 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The lowland capital of Susa, heavily influenced by Mesopotamian culture, coexisted with the highland core centred on <b>Anshan</b>, and this bifurcation meant that Elam could lose its lowlands to conquest while retaining an unconquered interior from which revival was always possible.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Gudea campaigned to the east against <b>Anshan</b> and Elam; his sixth year was named for the smiting of Anshan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 2004 BCE, Elamite forces captured and sacked Ur and carried Ibbi-Sin into captivity in <b>Anshan</b>, bringing the empire to an end.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Neo-Elamite kingdom weakened under Assyrian attack in the 7th century BCE, Persian rulers took control of <b>Anshan</b>, the old Elamite highland capital at Tall-i Malyan in Fars.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-anshan">Kingdom of Anshan</a></li><li><blockquote>After the obscure end of the Sukkalmah dynasty, a new line, the Kidinuids, ruled Susa and Anshan from about 1500 BCE with the title 'king of Susa and <b>Anshan</b>'.</blockquote><a href="/state/middle-elamite-kingdom">Middle Elamite kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
